  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You have two integers AAA and BBB, and you'd like to make them equal.\n\nTo this end, you can perform several moves. On the iii-th move,\n\n\nIf iii is odd, either add iii to AAA or add iii to BBB.\n\nNote that you aren't allowed to add iii to both, and must choose exactly one of them.\nIf iii is even, either subtract iii from AAA or subtract iii from BBB.\n\nOnce again, you aren't allowed to subtract from both, and must choose one.\nThat is,\n\n\nOn the first move, you add 111 to either AAA or BBB.\nOn the second move, you subtract 222 from either AAA or BBB.\nOn the third move, you add 333 to either AAA or BBB.\n\n⋮\\vdots⋮\nFind the **minimum** number of moves needed to make AAA and BBB equal.\n\nPrint −1-1−1 if they can never be made equal.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of a single line containing two space-separated integers AAA and BBB.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the answer: the minimum number of moves needed to make AAA equal BBB, or −1-1−1 if it's impossible.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤A,B≤1091 \\leq A, B \\leq 10^91≤A,B≤109"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n4 4\n5 4\n1 8\n1 9",
        "output": "0\n1\n5\n4",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: The numbers are already equal.\nTest case 222: Add 111 to BBB on the first move, making it 4+1=54+1 = 54+1=5. Both numbers are equal.\nTest case 333: Here's one sequence of moves:\n\nFirst, add 111 to BBB. The numbers are (1,9)(1, 9)(1,9).\nNext, subtract 222 from BBB. The numbers are (1,7)(1, 7)(1,7).\nThen, add 333 to BBB. The numbers are (1,10)(1, 10)(1,10).\nThen, subtract 444 from BBB. The numbers are (1,6)(1, 6)(1,6).\nFinally, add 555 to AAA. The numbers are (6,6)(6, 6)(6,6) and equal.\n\nIt can be proved that using four or fewer moves is impossible.\nTest case 444: One optimal sequence is as follows:\n\nAdd 111 to BBB.\nSubtract 222 from BBB.\nAdd 333 to AAA.\nSubtract 444 from BBB. The numbers are now (4,4)(4, 4)(4,4) and hence equal."
      }
    ]
  },
